    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="TX1_Written_Text"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="TXP8_has_component"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/P46i_forms_part_of"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Quantification of the inverse property: one to many (0,n:0,1)

Scope note of the inverse property:
This property is used to state the (physical) belonging of a glyph to a given TX1 Written Text.

Examples of the inverse property:
- The inscription (TX1) on the South face of the Arch of Constantine, has component the S-shaped glyph (TX9) engraved on the second line, representing the letter (TX8) “S” of the Latin writing system (TX3).

In First Order Logic of the inverse property:
- TXP8(x,y) ⇒ TX1(x)
- TXP8(x,y) ⇒ TX9(y)
- TXP8(x,y) ⇒ P46(x,y)
</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="TXP9_is_encoded_using">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">TXP9 is encoded using</rdfs:label>
    <skos:notation>TXP9</skos:notation>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">is encoded using</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="TX1_Written_Text"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="TX3_Writing_System"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="TXP9i_was_used_to_encode"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Quantification: many to many (0,n:0,n)

Scope note:
This property has the purpose of directly associating a TX1 Written Text with the TX3 Writing System used for encoding it. It is a shortcut of the more fully articulated path from TX1 Written Text through TXP5i was written by, TX2 Writing, TXP1 used writing system to TX3 Writing System.

Examples:
- The Gortyn Law inscriptions (TX1), engraved on the wall of the Amphitheatre of Gortyn (Crete), is encoded using the Greek alphabet (TX3).

In First Order Logic:
- TXP9(x,y) ⇒ TX1(x)
- TXP9(x,y) ⇒ TX3(y)
- TXP9(x,y) ⇔ (∃z)[TX2(z)] ˄ TXP5(z, x) ˄ TXP1(z, y)]
</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="TXP9i_was_used_to_encode">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">TXP9i was used to encode</rdfs:label>
    <skos:notation>TXP9i</skos:notation>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">was used to encode</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="TX3_Writing_System"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="TX1_Written_Text"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="TXP9_is_encoded_using"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Quantification of the inverse property: many to many (0,n:0,n)

Scope note of the inverse property:
This property has the purpose of directly associating a TX1 Written Text with the TX3 Writing System used for encoding it. It is a shortcut of the more fully articulated path from TX1 Written Text through TXP5i was written by, TX2 Writing, TXP1 used writing system to TX3 Writing System.

Examples of the inverse property:
- The Gortyn Law inscriptions (TX1), engraved on the wall of the Amphitheatre of Gortyn (Crete), is encoded using the Greek alphabet (TX3).

In First Order Logic of the inverse property:
- TXP9(x,y) ⇒ TX1(x)
- TXP9(x,y) ⇒ TX3(y)
- TXP9(x,y) ⇔ (∃z)[TX2(z)] ˄ TXP5(z, x) ˄ TXP1(z, y)]
</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="TXP10_deciphered_text">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">TXP10 deciphered text</rdfs:label>
    <skos:notation>TXP10</skos:notation>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">deciphered text</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="TX5_Text_Recognition"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="TXP10i_was_deciphered_by"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/O8_observed"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Quantification: one to one (0,1:0,n)

Scope note:
This property associates an instance of TX5 Text Recognition with an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing carrying a glyph or a sequence of glyphs that was recognized in the respective activity of text recognition. Typically, the associated instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing is more specifically an instance of TX1 Written Text, however, a text may also be recognized from a mechanical copy, a photograph, squeeze or other form of material copy of a written original, which would not by itself constitute an instance of TX1 Written Text. In the latter case, the material copy should be associated with the original written text using the property ‘P130 shows features’.
If the text was actually recognized only from a digital representation, this property should not be used, rather the property TXP13 deciphered via the representation should be used instead.

Examples:
- The autoptic investigation (TX5) carried out by Rodolfo Lanciani between 1893 and 1901, deciphered the South inscription (TX1) on the Arch of Constantine.

In First Order Logic:
- TXP10(x,y) ⇒ TX5(x)
- TXP10(x,y) ⇒ E24(y)
- TXP10(x,y) ⇒ O8(x,y)
- TXP10(x, z1) ∧ TXP14(x, z2) ⇒ P130(z1, z2)
</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="TXP10i_was_deciphered_by">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">TXP10i was deciphered by</rdfs:label>
    <skos:notation>TXP10i</skos:notation>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">was deciphered by</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/cidoc-crm/E24_Physical_Human-Made_Thing"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="TX5_Text_Recognition"/>
    <owl:inverseOf rdf:resource="TXP10_deciphered_text"/>
    <rdfs:subPropertyOf rdf:resource="http://www.cidoc-crm.org/extensions/crmsci/O8i_was_observed_by"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Quantification of the inverse property: one to one (0,1:0,n)

Scope note of the inverse property:
This property associates an instance of TX5 Text Recognition with an instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing carrying a glyph or a sequence of glyphs that was recognized in the respective activity of text recognition. Typically, the associated instance of E24 Physical Human-Made Thing is more specifically an instance of TX1 Written Text, however, a text may also be recognized from a mechanical copy, a photograph, squeeze or other form of material copy of a written original, which would not by itself constitute an instance of TX1 Written Text. In the latter case, the material copy should be associated with the original written text using the property ‘P130 shows features’.
If the text was actually recognized only from a digital representation, this property should not be used, rather the property TXP13 deciphered via the representation should be used instead.

Examples of the inverse property:
- The autoptic investigation (TX5) carried out by Rodolfo Lanciani between 1893 and 1901, deciphered the South inscription (TX1) on the Arch of Constantine.

In First Order Logic of the inverse property:
- TXP10(x,y) ⇒ TX5(x)
- TXP10(x,y) ⇒ E24(y)
- TXP10(x,y) ⇒ O8(x,y)
- TXP10(x, z1) ∧ TXP14(x, z2) ⇒ P130(z1, z2)
</rdfs:comment>
  </owl:ObjectProperty>
  <owl:ObjectProperty rdf:about="TXP11_transcribed">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">TXP11 transcribed</rdfs:label>
    <skos:notation>TXP11</skos:notation>
    <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">transcribed</skos:prefLabel>
    <rdfs:domain rdf:resource="TX6_Transliteration"/>
    <rdfs:range rdf:resource="TX12_Grapheme_Sequence"/>
